# Authentication in AdvisorPilot

Three concurrent auth paths. Code that touches auth MUST handle all three or be explicit about which subset it supports.

| Path | Where the credential lives | When used |
|---|---|---|
| **NextAuth (Google)** | `next-auth.session-token` cookie | Advisors who signed in via "Sign in with Google" |
| **Supabase email/password** | `sessionStorage["ap_supabase_at"]` (access token) + `["ap_supabase_rt"]` (refresh token), sent as `Authorization: Bearer <jwt>` | Advisors who signed in via the email/password form |
| **Upload token** | URL path `/client-upload/[token]` | Public client-side magic-link uploads (separate flow; not relevant to advisor APIs) |

## Server-side: every authenticated API route uses `resolveAdvisorIdentity`

`lib/advisor-auth.ts` exports `resolveAdvisorIdentity(req)`. It checks BOTH the NextAuth session AND the `Authorization` header, returns `{ email, userId, provider } | null`. Use it. Don't roll your own.

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD
import { resolveAdvisorIdentity } from "@/lib/advisor-auth";

export const GET = async (req: Request) => {
  const identity = await resolveAdvisorIdentity(req);
  if (!identity) return NextResponse.json({ error: "Sign in." }, { status: 401 });
  // ...use identity.email
};

// ❌ BAD — only handles NextAuth (Google) users; email/password users get 401
const session = await getServerSession(authOptions);
if (!session) return NextResponse.json({ error: "Sign in." }, { status: 401 });
```

## Client-side: every authenticated fetch uses `advisorFetch`

`lib/advisor-fetch.ts` exports `advisorFetch()`. It reads the Bearer token from `sessionStorage`, sets the `Authorization` header, sends `credentials: "include"` for cookies, AND auto-refreshes on 401. Use it. Plain `fetch()` only works for Google users — email/password users will silently 401.

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD
import { advisorFetch } from "@/lib/advisor-fetch";

const res = await advisorFetch("/api/clients?limit=200", { cache: "no-store" });
if (res.status === 401) { /* render "session expired" UI */ return; }

// ❌ BAD — works for Google users, breaks for email/password users
const res = await fetch("/api/clients?limit=200");
```

## Server Components CANNOT see browser sessionStorage

A Server Component that fetches an authenticated API route via plain `fetch()` works for Google users (cookies forward automatically) but **fails for email/password users** because their JWT lives in browser `sessionStorage` — the server has no access to it.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — RSC fetching authenticated API; email/password users get 401
export default async function ClientDetailPage({ params }) {
  const { id } = await params;
  const res = await fetch(`http://${host}/api/clients/${id}`, {
    headers: { cookie: incomingCookie },  // only NextAuth cookies; no Bearer
  });
  // ...
}

// ✅ GOOD — thin RSC hands off to a Client Component that uses advisorFetch
export default async function ClientDetailPage({ params }) {
  const { id, tab } = await params;
  return <ClientDetailContent clientId={id} tab={tab} />;  // client component
}
```

When in doubt about whether a route needs to support email/password users, **fetch client-side via `advisorFetch`**.

## Don't add `getServerSession` redirects to layouts

Server-side auth gates that assume a NextAuth session create redirect loops for email/password users (no NextAuth cookie → redirect to /login → after login → back to layout → still no NextAuth cookie → redirect to /login → infinite loop).

The existing convention: API routes return 401, client surfaces show a "session expired" prompt with a link to `/login`. Don't redirect from layouts.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — redirect loop for email/password users
export default async function CrmLayout({ children }) {
  const session = await getServerSession(authOptions);
  if (!session?.user?.email) redirect("/login");
  return <CrmShell>{children}</CrmShell>;
}

// ✅ GOOD — let API enforce auth; client handles 401 gracefully
export default function CrmLayout({ children }) {
  return <CrmShell>{children}</CrmShell>;
}
```

## Quick checklist before merging

- [ ] Authenticated API route → calls `resolveAdvisorIdentity(req)` at the top
- [ ] Client-side fetch to authenticated API → uses `advisorFetch()`, not plain `fetch()`
- [ ] RSC fetch to authenticated API → only if you're sure the route's consumers are Google-only; otherwise convert to Client Component + `advisorFetch`
- [ ] Layout/Page-level auth gating → don't add `getServerSession` redirects; rely on the API + client surface

## Why this rule exists

A Phase 1 PR called plain `fetch()` from the Roster + RSC fetch from the client-detail page. Email/password users got 401 on every CRM API call. Then a "fix" added `getServerSession` to the CRM layout, which created a redirect loop and locked the email/password user out entirely. Both mistakes would have been caught by reading `lib/advisor-fetch.ts` and `lib/advisor-auth.ts` first.
